{:edb/db-before-t 2517 :edb/db-after-t 2518 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535830 50 #inst "2026-10-15T00:35:51.937Z" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186054786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531646 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186063791 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063793 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531571 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531632 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531603 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531562 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531561 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531567 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531569 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531588 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531618 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531629 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064359 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531617 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064956 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[- 2, 2]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186064956 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186064956 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has corner points at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=\\pm 1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$ Hence, $t(x)$  is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065572 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065573 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065574 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065575 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065576 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531058 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531102 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531139 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531236 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531252 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186065571 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531058 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066105 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530895 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066105 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530895 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066106 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531007 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066106 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531007 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531018 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066130 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531018 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531216 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186066131 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531216 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068481 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations\n\n$$\ny=5x+3\n$$\n\nand $y=x-1$. So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{y = 5x + 3; y = x - 1\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation\n\n$$\ny=5x+3\n$$\n\ninto the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 3 &= x - 1 \\\\\n5x &= x - 4 \\\\\n4x &= -4 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 1 \\\\\n&=-1 - 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\, y=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2)$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068481 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+3 \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+3$ into the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ 5x & =x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1, \\, y=-2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068482 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068482 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068482 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ 2x-y=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n![](images/d2/d2cac87bca62cf4fa8cc6ad3c0bd289de7cb0f8c39e742dd6e7731cb90b0e354.svg)\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{2y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ (x+4x) & =(1+14) \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation $x+2y=1$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =1 \\\\ 3+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=3, \\, y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068482 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; 2x - y = 7\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; 2x - y = 7 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= 14 \\\\\n(x + 4x) &= (1 + 14) \\\\\n5x &= 15 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=1\n$$\n\nto solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n3 + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n2y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=3, \\, y=-1\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1)$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068483 1071 "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4)$. Find the parameters $a$ and $b$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068483 1071 "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068483 1074 "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values\n\n$$\nx=b, \\, y=-4\n$$\n\nmust solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{y =-3x + a; y = x - 1\n$$\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nx=b, \\, y=-4\n$$\n\nto obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n{-4 =-3b + a; - 4 = b - 1\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= b - 1 \\\\\n- 3 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= -3b + a \\\\\n- 4 &= -3(-3) + a \\\\\n- 4 &= 9 + a \\\\\n- 13 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068483 1074 "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b, \\, y=-4$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+a \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b, \\, y=-4$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=-3b+a \\\\ -4=b-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =b-1 \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-3b+a \\\\ -4 & =-3(-3)+a \\\\ -4 & =9+a \\\\ -13 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068593 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} .$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068593 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2}$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068593 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} &=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 3} {x \\cdot y^2} &=\\dfrac {6} {xy^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068593 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} \n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 3} {x \\cdot y^2}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {6} {xy^2}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068594 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {6a} {b^2} = x$ and $\\dfrac {3b} {2a}= y$, what is $x\\cdot y$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068594 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {6a} {b^2} = x$ and $\\dfrac {3b} {2a}= y,$ what is $x\\cdot y?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068594 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot y &= \\frac{6a}{b^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{3b}{2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6a \\cdot 3b}{b^{2} \\cdot 2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18ab}{2ab^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068594 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot y & =\\frac{6a}{{b}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{3b}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{6a\\cdot 3b}{{b}^{2}\\cdot 2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{18ab}{2a{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068595 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {(n + 1)(n + 3)} {4n^2} \\cdot \\dfrac {8n}{(n + 3)(n - 2)}$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068595 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {(n + 1)(n + 3)} {4n^2} \\cdot \\dfrac {8n}{(n + 3)(n - 2)}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068595 1074 "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n^{2}) \\cdot (8n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) &= ((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n \\cdot n) \\cdot (2 \\cdot 4n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{n + 1}{n} \\cdot \\frac{2}{n - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2n + 2}{n^{2} - 2n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068595 1074 "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)(n+3)}{4{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{8n}{(n+3)(n-2)} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cancel{(n+3)}}{\\cancel{4n}\\cdot n}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{4n}}{\\cancel{(n+3)}(n-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{n+1}{n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2n+2}{{n}^{2}-2n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068596 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4}$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068596 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068596 1074 "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} &=\\dfrac {3(p - 4)} {(p + 3)(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3\\cancel{(p - 4)}} {\\cancel{(p + 3)}(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\cancel{p + 3}} {\\cancel{p - 4}} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3}{p - 3} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068596 1074 "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3(p - 4)} {(p + 3)(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3\\cancel{(p - 4)}} {\\cancel{(p + 3)}(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\cancel{p + 3}} {\\cancel{p - 4}} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3}{p - 3}\n\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068657 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y - 2y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2y(1 - y) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$, and $y=1$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}yf(y)dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y(2y - 2y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 4π\\int_{0}^{1}(y^{2} - y^{3})dy \\\\\n&= 4π(\\int_{0}^{1}y^{2}dy - \\int_{0}^{1}y^{3}dy) \\\\\n&= \\left.4π(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{1} - \\left.\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{1}) \\\\\n&= 4π((\\frac{1}{3} - 0) - (\\frac{1}{4} - 0)) \\\\\n&= 4π(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068657 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068658 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny(4 - y) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0, \\, 4$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=4$.\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}yf(y)dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{4}y(4y - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186068658 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(4-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0, \\, 4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531570 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531619 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531659 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531558 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531585 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531564 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531607 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531601 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531623 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531636 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531654 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531572 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531580 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531610 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531650 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531574 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531576 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531602 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531612 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531625 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531599 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531622 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531634 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531648 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068945 1050 780.0 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068950 1050 540.0 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186068966 1050 480.0 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069023 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x = y - 2y^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069023 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=y-2{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069023 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(1-2y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069112 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $y = 3x - 1$ and $y =-4x + 6$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069112 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069112 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-1 \\\\ y=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069128 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $x - y = 7$ and $3x + 2y = 11$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069128 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069128 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y=11 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=14 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069746 1061 33070 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069746 1061 643617 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069747 1061 33074 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069747 1061 643622 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069748 1061 33076 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069748 1061 643621 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069749 1061 33082 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069749 1061 643618 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069972 1064 "Multiplying rational expressions is just like multiplying fractions. After all, fractions are just rational expressions with constants!\n\nFor example, to multiply the rational expressions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nwe multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\cdot \\dfrac{y}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\cdot y}{x \\cdot 2} = \\dfrac{3y}{2x}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069972 1064 "Multiplying rational expressions is just like multiplying fractions. After all, fractions are just rational expressions with constants!\n\nFor example, to multiply the rational expressions $\\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $\\dfrac{y}{2},$ we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\cdot \\dfrac{y}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\cdot y}{x \\cdot 2} = \\dfrac{3y}{2x}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069988 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $x=f(y)$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$, as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/19/1927064dcef318f2c0490ff071532b024da30a328b2752c28e197171eeec6b5f.png)\n\nOne way of computing the volume of the solid is to divide the interval from $y=c$ to $y=d$ on the $y$-axis to get horizontal slices. This approach is called the **shell method**.\n\nConsider one such slice of height $\\Delta{y}$, as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a solid **shell** (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/25/2561448ab909594dda427b76860fcdda5147f8966fec9e20958d1c5fca11b536.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $y$. The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{y}$ and its height is $x=f(y)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its width is $x=f(y)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{y}$, and its height is $2\\pi y$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/03/03030fb80f0aa51c637e6d39b4f763e8d7fc89abd111a012d3e560a952cfba99.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\nV_{shell} \\mid \\approx V_{rec}; = 2πy \\cdot x \\cdot Δy; = 2πyf(y)Δy\n$$\n\nThe sum over all the shells will approximate the total volume that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{y} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}yxdy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}yf(y)dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069988 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $x=f(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/19/1927064dcef318f2c0490ff071532b024da30a328b2752c28e197171eeec6b5f.png)\n\nOne way of computing the volume of the solid is to divide the interval from $y=c$ to $y=d$ on the $y$-axis to get horizontal slices. This approach is called the **shell method**.\n\nConsider one such slice of height $\\Delta{y},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a solid **shell** (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/25/2561448ab909594dda427b76860fcdda5147f8966fec9e20958d1c5fca11b536.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $y.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{y}$ and its height is $x=f(y)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its width is $x=f(y)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{y},$ and its height is $2\\pi y$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/03/03030fb80f0aa51c637e6d39b4f763e8d7fc89abd111a012d3e560a952cfba99.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi y\\cdot x\\cdot Δy \\\\  & =2\\pi yf(y)Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum over all the shells will approximate the total volume that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{y} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y\\,f(y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186069989 1064 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/1d/1d23dde2cdd557feb50f82f72f4ff09174d5fb9a0c94e7810a6c06acb95a13d0.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}yf(y)dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y(2y - 2y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}2y^{2} - 2y^{3}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x$, one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2x},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2x},\\end{vmatrix}y < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f4/f416ab43297265aec4836eca2020d8f08976852cc025ad4d3a0766023187adef.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186069989 1064 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/1d/1d23dde2cdd557feb50f82f72f4ff09174d5fb9a0c94e7810a6c06acb95a13d0.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x,$ one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n![](images/d9/d957a8e263db8ddd8ad3e5fa37f63b0cdbb47e706c367350c3ff6b8ff90294ff.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f416ab43297265aec4836eca2020d8f08976852cc025ad4d3a0766023187adef.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186070021 1064 "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = 2x + 1\n$$\n\nA sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=2x+1\n$$\n\nSo, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{y = x + 2; y = 2x + 1\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation\n\n$$\ny=2x+1\n$$\n\ngives an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\nx + 1 &= 2x \\\\\n1 &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x + 2 \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=1, \\, y=3\n$$\n\nSo, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3)$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186070021 1064 "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines $y = x + 2$ and $y = 2x + 1.$ A sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=x+2$ and $y=2x+1.$ So, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=x+2$ into the second equation $y=2x+1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =2x \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1, \\, y=3.$ So, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531591 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186073821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531586 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186073900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531593 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186085290 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085294 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186087823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531604 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186087823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531635 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186087824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531579 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186087824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531613 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186087901 1050 540.0 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186088107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186088107 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y = 8^{x} + 0.5$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186088107 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={8}^{x}+0.5?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186088107 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={8}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d1/d1d90ab26cf9c3939b355a781b2192f962204b077b990b6a142db642a1316f4f.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={8}^{x}+0.5,$ we take the graph of $y={8}^{x}$ and shift it $0.5$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,1.5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=0.5.$\n\n![](images/45/452f0482708656146b2e5cd7f58832250fc95e25707cacb66800255aee977b21.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186088868 1064 "Adding a value to $y=2^x$ will **translate** (shift) the exponential function up or down the $y$-axis. \n\nFor instance, adding $3$ to $y=2^x$ will move each point on the exponential function $3$ units up.\n\n![](images/c8/c89fd5fdcdc3773143a5f14ecb20f5bd2917bce20ece18a7dc70631bf358a0e8.png)\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The $y$-intercept $(0,1)$ has moved up $3$ units to the point $(0,4).$\n\n- The horizontal asymptote $y=0$ has moved up $3$ units to $y=3.$\n\nAs a second example, let's consider the function $y=2^x-3.$ In this case, the graph of $y=2^x$ is shifted *down* by $3$ units.\n\n![](images/84/84987b3e9138a2e7e8afe70a7aa28a1cd81e081033aa2a766773e63cb8f41da5.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186088868 1064 "Adding a value to $y=2^x$ will **translate** (shift) the exponential function up or down the $y$-axis. \n\nFor instance, adding $3$ to $y=2^x$ will move each point on the exponential function $3$ units up.\n\n![](images/c8/c89fd5fdcdc3773143a5f14ecb20f5bd2917bce20ece18a7dc70631bf358a0e8.png)\n\nNote the following points:\n\n1\\. The $y$-intercept $(0,1)$ has moved up $3$ units to the point $(0,4).$\n\n2\\. The horizontal asymptote $y=0$ has moved up $3$ units to $y=3.$\n\nAs a second example, let's consider the function $y=2^x-3.$ In this case, the graph of $y=2^x$ is shifted *down* by $3$ units.\n\n![](images/84/84987b3e9138a2e7e8afe70a7aa28a1cd81e081033aa2a766773e63cb8f41da5.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186091739 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186091740 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186091741 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186091743 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531082 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531239 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531253 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186091738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531306 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186091738 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531239 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531626 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531565 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531606 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531628 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531644 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092923 1071 "![](images/fa/fa58c9d1c0680dd94bb25c04e0a268df836e14bfb217f0a20d3bd75224cec4e8.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r = e^{θ}$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186092923 1071 "![](images/fa/fa58c9d1c0680dd94bb25c04e0a268df836e14bfb217f0a20d3bd75224cec4e8.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186092923 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186096677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531557 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186096677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531647 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531559 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531630 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186100942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531568 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104471 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104472 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104473 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104474 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104475 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530919 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186104470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531069 13194139535830 1