e following statements are true?\n\nI. $E$, $G$ and $H$ are collinear\n\nII. $A$, $B$ and $G$ are **not** collinear\n\nIII. $B$, $F$ and $H$ are collinear" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186111394 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/fb/fb82255ac578efc90bb070dab669a19950387cab7d9011de4d5d0d94dd9de505.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c20ebe076b3e655b7709ccb337c68d4aa8505bfabdb5a8755e5a1410642d612.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/98/9859ea0144923d69e37d64130910608f6fc3e7afe09d74af19a5402030e0d3cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186129318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531587 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135605 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135606 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135607 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135608 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135609 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530900 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531013 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531192 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186135604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531198 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [175921861356