0 17592186045417 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531621 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531584 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531590 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531594 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531652 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531581 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531615 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531575 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531578 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531596 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186166937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531573 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167169 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3/2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1/2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167169 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2t + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n$x_{c}(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t) = x_{c}(t) +$ |  | $3/2$ |  | $t +$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $1/2$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186167169 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+3{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}+1) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2)t+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0 \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}+1={a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+1={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2},\\,{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-1,\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3/2 \\\\ -1/2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1/2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167173 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)-{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+{e}^{3t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167173 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) + 2e^{3t}; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) - e^{3t}$\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| $x^{′}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $x(t) + e^{3t}$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186167173 1074 "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+0\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186167990 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n![](images/03/037289f6f3a779d750cef23dc18d149786604bd477565e3708d6470f2e3c2f7a.svg)\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n![](images/77/77fc846681e1f0a98675b06f962e42f4491fd07d9bb2eab291e69cd49e4dd5f9.svg)\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n![](images/47/47fb751641455ab4cf85756166fa8811bd8fe3ddc6bcb6005c8e6be8c31b6fe1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f4/f47e1272123fb8f4a5f54931929b9492f54e9d78d590b2ee28394bf29976ce44.svg)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186167990 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n![](images/03/037289f6f3a779d750cef23dc18d149786604bd477565e3708d6470f2e3c2f7a.svg)\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n![](images/77/77fc846681e1f0a98675b06f962e42f4491fd07d9bb2eab291e69cd49e4dd5f9.svg)\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n![](images/47/47fb751641455ab4cf85756166fa8811bd8fe3ddc6bcb6005c8e6be8c31b6fe1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f4/f47e1272123fb8f4a5f54931929b9492f54e9d78d590b2ee28394bf29976ce44.svg)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186171934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531639 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173345 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173346 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173347 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173348 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173349 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531108 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531154 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531257 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531305 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531374 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173344 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531257 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173357 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173358 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173359 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173360 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173361 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531041 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531075 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531278 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531292 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531363 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186173356 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531041 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186175036 1075 #edb/ref 17592186175039 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186175036 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531134 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186175036 1078 #edb/ref 17592186175040 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186175964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531592 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186175965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531638 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176119 1050 420.0 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176181 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $2,$ we must have $r=2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r=2.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176183 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176483 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{(-49)^{2}}$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186176483 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{{(-49)}^{2}}?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176483 1074 "Remember that by definition, the square root is always zero or positive, never negative.\n\nSo, the square root comes out to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-49)^{2}}=\\sqrt{{49}^{2}}=49.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176973 1064 "In a rectangular (or Cartesian) system of coordinates, we can think of a point as the vertex of a rectangular solid. For instance, the point $P(1,\\sqrt3,3)$ can be represented as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47873274f1cc630a8275b3ef2f83210b3d16a4f3d0acabeae9dbda32844a9adf.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we consider the corresponding cylindrical coordinates of $P,$ given by $\\left(2, \\dfrac{\\pi}3, 3\\right)$ in this case, we can think of $P$ as a point that lies on the edge of a cylinder, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fefa7c5fac5cac803fdebeaa9eab4233d723b452d2ee8dc17151d992064223f.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176973 1064 "In a rectangular (or Cartesian) system of coordinates, we can think of a point as the vertex of a rectangular solid. For instance, the point $P(1,\\sqrt3,3)$ can be represented as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47873274f1cc630a8275b3ef2f83210b3d16a4f3d0acabeae9dbda32844a9adf.png)\n\nWhen we consider the corresponding cylindrical coordinates of $P,$ given by $\\left(2, \\dfrac{\\pi}3, 3\\right)$ in this case, we can think of $P$ as a point that lies on the edge of a cylinder, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fefa7c5fac5cac803fdebeaa9eab4233d723b452d2ee8dc17151d992064223f.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186176986 1064 "What surface is defined by the equation $z^2=r^2$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?\n\nAgain, recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz^2=r^2\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis.\nIn other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis, which is given by $\\dfrac{r}{z}$, must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} =\\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{z^2}} = \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{r^2}} = \\pm1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\n- For $\\dfrac{r}{z} = 1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for positive $z{:}$\n\n![](images/18/1841d50bb07c0e6787f4824af0e13516912fb9ad77fac0515f00c243899eb18e.png)\n\n \n\n- On the other hand, for $\\dfrac{r}{z} = -1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for negative $z{:}$\n\n![](images/e3/e32b1b4ba84faea7a4dc536e0f49bcda9295a4a42790162b3b9bad06db39a908.png)\n\nTherefore, combining the two half cones, the equation $z^2 = r^2$ defines the *cone* shown below.\n\n![](images/84/842f8cf967d10144e568804bdfb1aadf75f029cf87dd7b6785168a44a95d0615.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186176986 1064 "What surface is defined by the equation $z^2=r^2$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?\n\nAgain, recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz^2=r^2\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis.\nIn other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis, which is given by $\\dfrac{r}{z}$, must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} =\\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{z^2}} = \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{r^2}} = \\pm1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\n- For $\\dfrac{r}{z} = 1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for positive $z{:}$\n\n![](images/18/1841d50bb07c0e6787f4824af0e13516912fb9ad77fac0515f00c243899eb18e.png)\n\n- On the other hand, for $\\dfrac{r}{z} = -1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for negative $z{:}$\n\n![](images/e3/e32b1b4ba84faea7a4dc536e0f49bcda9295a4a42790162b3b9bad06db39a908.png)\n\nTherefore, combining the two half cones, the equation $z^2 = r^2$ defines the *cone* shown below.\n\n![](images/84/842f8cf967d10144e568804bdfb1aadf75f029cf87dd7b6785168a44a95d0615.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186176987 1064 "So far, we have kept the domains of the plane polar coordinates restricted to $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$ What happens if we lift these restrictions?\n\nConsider the equation $r=\\theta$ with the domain restrictions $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ which defines the surface shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84737b12a1689fe2ac03ab09572e4c396d35493f55df5d65af80eeb8c5ae5676.png)\n\nSuppose we remove the restriction $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ so that it can be any number. What would the surface look like then?\n\nIf we remove the restriction on $\\theta,$ we obtain more spirals. For example, the diagram below shows $r=\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 4\\pi).$\n\n![](images/56/568291daf47340265d97a121f0b7b927233f2698e87e0d5adebbba640ff20afe.png)\n\nWe can also lift the restriction $r \\geq 0$ so $r$ can take negative values. Typically, allowing negative values of $r$ adds $\\pi$ to the parameter $\\theta.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(|r|,\\theta ),\\, & r\\ge 0, \\\\ (|r|,\\theta +\\pi ),\\, & r<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186176987 1064 "So far, we have kept the domains of the plane polar coordinates restricted to $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$ What happens if we lift these restrictions?\n\nConsider the equation $r=\\theta$ with the domain restrictions $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ which defines the surface shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84737b12a1689fe2ac03ab09572e4c396d35493f55df5d65af80eeb8c5ae5676.png)\n\nSuppose we remove the restriction $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ so that it can be any number. What would the surface look 