,$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186204588 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $3\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 2$ matrix is a $3\\times 2$ matrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186204590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186204590 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x) = Ax$ and $f(y) = By$, where $x ∈ R^{3}, y ∈ R^{2}$, and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is $\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x))$ evaluated at $x_{0} = [0,-1, 1]^{T}$?\n\n| $\\left.\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x))\\right\\|_{x_{0}} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $6$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |  |" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186204590 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{3},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,-1,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186204590 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205190 1064 "Consider the multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n \\to\\mathbb R$, defined by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf y) = f(y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n)\n$$\n\nwith $n$ independent variables, and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R^n$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(x) \\\\ y_{2}(x) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ y_{n}(x)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a vector-valued function of a variable $x$.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the derivative of $f(\\mathbf{y}(x))$ with respect to $x$ is given by the multivariable chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_1}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_2}{\\partial x} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_n} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_n}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis can be written as a product of matrices as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂y_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂y_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that this can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}}_{1\\times n} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}}_{n\\times 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}$ is the *total derivative* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf y$, which is a row vector of size $1\\times n$, and\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is the *total derivative* of $\\mathbf y$ with respect to $x$, which is a column vector of size $n\\times 1$.\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this matrix formulation to a concrete example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205190 1064 "Consider the multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n \\to\\mathbb R,$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf y) = f(y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n)\n$$\n\nwith $n$ independent variables, and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R^n,$ defined by\n\n![](images/af/afa5940765a9d39d7f8ab5aeefd4b550434adc0497b1b65845b00e30dd83d1f5.svg)\n\nwhich is a vector-valued function of a variable $x.$ \n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the derivative of $f(\\mathbf{y}(x))$ with respect to $x$ is given by the multivariable chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_1}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_2}{\\partial x} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_n} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_n}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis can be written as a product of matrices as follows:\n\n![](images/14/1482285b6db08d8b944fd98871736ffebd912d78842ceac6fbad959b9b9e9e6d.svg)\n\nNow, notice that this can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x}\n= \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}}_{1\\times n} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}}_{n\\times 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}$ is the *total derivative* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf y$, which is a row vector of size $1\\times n$, and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is the *total derivative* of $\\mathbf y$ with respect to $x$, which is a column vector of size $n\\times 1.$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this matrix formulation to a concrete example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205191 1064 "Let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ be two differentiable vector-valued functions. We want to compute the derivative of the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nThe **chain rule for total derivatives** tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{m \\times n} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}\n$$\n\nHere, the dot denotes matrix multiplication. The inner matrix dimensions match, so this is a valid matrix product.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying the chain rule for total derivatives, the *order of multiplication matters!* For example, if we swap the order of the matrix products, we'd get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q} \\qquad \\text{(WRONG!)}\n$$\n\nThis is incorrect because the inner matrix dimensions do not agree, making it an invalid matrix product.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205191 1064 "Let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ be two differentiable vector-valued functions. We want to compute the derivative of the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nThe **chain rule for total derivatives** tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{m \\times n}\n=\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nHere, the dot denotes matrix multiplication. The inner matrix dimensions match, so this is a valid matrix product.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying the chain rule for total derivatives, the *order of multiplication matters!* For example, if we swap the order of the matrix products, we'd get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} = \n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q}\n\\qquad \\text{(WRONG!)}\n$$\n\nThis is incorrect because the inner matrix dimensions do not agree, making it an invalid matrix product.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205198 1064 "Suppose we define\n![](images/11/11259475d7bcdbbdf2d28e6540f1af92282a188d05dd6d70038b00ec5ab611a5.svg)\n\nThen, we have $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2.$ \n\nLet's see how to compute the derivative for the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \n= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}\n\\cdot\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is also a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nComputing our derivatives, we get\n![](images/91/918c4e1af2c1f33f6d056843ee0d038b4b91dc67e8c112c899a05c273d5fcd89.svg)\n\nThen, by the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matrix gives us the total derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205198 1064 "Suppose we define\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} + y_{2} \\\\ y_{1} - 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we have $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nLet's see how to compute the derivative for the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$.\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is also a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nComputing our derivatives, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, by the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 2x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{1} & -4x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matrix gives us the total derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x$." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205199 1064 "Suppose we have the following differentiable functions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^p, \\qquad\n\n\\mathbf{z}: \\mathbb{R}^p \\to \\mathbb{R}^q, \\qquad \\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m\n$$\n\nThe chain rule can be extended in the natural way as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) \n= \n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z}}_{m\\times q} \n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y}}_{q\\times p} \n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}}_{p\\times n}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205199 1064 "Suppose we have the following differentiable functions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^p, \\qquad \\mathbf{z}: \\mathbb{R}^p \\to \\mathbb{R}^q, \\qquad \\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m\n$$\n\nThe chain rule can be extended in the natural way as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z}}_{m\\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y}}_{q\\times p} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}}_{p\\times n}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205217 1064 "Let's *verify* that our result from the previous slide is correct by computing the derivative directly.\n\nRecall that\n![](images/3e/3e421d8e8fdac73c75fbec18f98a7b338ac46083ea0c4d07cd8f07121420dac1.svg)\n\nFirst, we substitute $\\mathbf{y}$ into $\\mathbf{f}$ to write $\\mathbf{f}$ explicitly in terms of $\\mathbf{x}{:}$\n![](images/58/58f11371168007d979c4ffeae1e6643441459b015d7cf7a08e60c5b05e4f93a1.svg) \n\nNow, we compute the derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis agrees with the result we obtained using the chain rule." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205217 1064 "Let's *verify* that our result from the previous slide is correct by computing the derivative directly.\n\nRecall that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} + y_{2} \\\\ y_{1} - 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we substitute $\\mathbf{y}$ into $\\mathbf{f}$ to write $\\mathbf{f}$ explicitly in terms of $\\mathbf{x}{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} \\\\ x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 2x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{1} & -4x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis agrees with the result we obtained using the chain rule." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205218 1064 "We can use the chain rule for functions that use matrices in their definition.\n\nFor example, consider the functions\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2$, and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))\n$$\n\nevaluated at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_0 = [2, \\, 0, \\, -1]^T\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{B}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = \\mathbf{B}$.\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}$.\n\nSince these derivatives are constant matrices, the value at $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is simply their product. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x)) &= \\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= BA \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186205218 1064 "We can use the chain rule for functions that use matrices in their definition.\n\nFor example, consider the functions $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y},$ where $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n![](images/c7/c721b41403d149ac6d97e543aabb22dec2ce200c99e4dccdae09204eb520b555.svg)\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = [2, \\, 0, \\, -1]^T?$\n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{B}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = \\mathbf{B}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nSince these derivatives are constant matrices, the value at $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is simply their product. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205396 1078 #edb/ref 17592186205400 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205402 1078 #edb/ref 17592186205407 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530883 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205414 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530883 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205415 1078 #edb/ref 17592186205418 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205421 1078 #edb/ref 17592186205425 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531080 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205427 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531080 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186205428 1078 #edb/ref 17592186205429 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208276 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $u$, $v$, and $w$ with probabilities $0.2$, $0.5$, and $0.3$, respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=u] = 6, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=v] = 9, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=w] = 3$.\n\nWhat is $\\textrm{E}[X]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208276 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ with probabilities $0.2,$ $0.5,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=u] = 6, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=v] = 9, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=w] = 3.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\textrm{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208276 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y$, along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_y \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y = u$ with probability $0.2$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = u] = 6$\n- $Y = v$ with probability $0.5$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = v] = 9$\n- $Y = w$ with probability $0.3$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = w] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[X∣Y = u] \\cdot P(Y = u) + E[X∣Y = v] \\cdot P(Y = v) \\\\\n&= + E[X∣Y = w] \\cdot P(Y = w) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 0.2 + 9 \\cdot 0.5 + 3 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 1.2 + 4.5 + 0.9 \\\\\n&= 6.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208276 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_y \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y = u$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = u] = 6$\n\n- $Y = v$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = v] = 9$\n\n- $Y = w$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = w] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=u]\\cdot P(Y=u)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=v]\\cdot P(Y=v) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=w]\\cdot P(Y=w) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.2+9\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.2+4.5+0.9 \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208277 1071 "A wildlife researcher is sent to either Forest $1$ or Forest $2$ to record animal sightings. With probability $0.7$, he is sent to Forest $1$, where the expected number of sightings in a day is $6.2$. With probability $0.3$, he is sent to Forest $2$, where the expected number is $3.8$.\n\nWhat is the expected number of animal sightings in a day?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208277 1071 "A wildlife researcher is sent to either Forest $1$ or Forest $2$ to record animal sightings. With probability $0.7,$ he is sent to Forest $1,$ where the expected number of sightings in a day is $6.2.$ With probability $0.3,$ he is sent to Forest $2,$ where the expected number is $3.8.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of animal sightings in a day?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208277 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of animal sightings the researcher records daily. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the forest where the researcher is stationed.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of animal sightings in a day, and let $Y$ be the forest to which the researcher is assigned.\n\n- $Y = 1$ with probability $0.7$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 1] = 6.2$.\n- $Y = 2$ with probability $0.3$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 2] = 3.8$.\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[X∣Y = 1] \\cdot P(Y = 1) + E[X∣Y = 2] \\cdot P(Y = 2) \\\\\n&= 6.2 \\cdot 0.7 + 3.8 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 4.34 + 1.14 \\\\\n&= 5.48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208277 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of animal sightings the researcher records daily. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the forest where the researcher is stationed.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of animal sightings in a day, and let $Y$ be the forest to which the researcher is assigned.\n\n- $Y = 1$ with probability $0.7,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 1] = 6.2.$\n\n- $Y = 2$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 2] = 3.8.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =6.2\\cdot 0.7+3.8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =4.34+1.14 \\\\  & =5.48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208278 1071 "Let $Y$ be a discrete random variable, and suppose $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = 3Y + 2$. If $\\textrm{E}[X] = 20$, what is $\\textrm{E}[Y]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208278 1071 "Let $Y$ be a discrete random variable, and suppose $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = 3Y + 2.$ If $\\textrm{E}[X] = 20,$ what is $\\textrm{E}[Y]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208278 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ in terms of $Y$, and the value of $\\textrm{E}[X]$. This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[3Y + 2]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[3Y + 2] \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot E[Y] + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = 20\n$$\n\nso we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 &= 3 \\cdot E[Y] + 2 \\\\\n18 &= 3 \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\nE[Y] &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208278 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[3Y + 2].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[3Y+2] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 20,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+2 \\\\ 18 & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208279 1071 "In a fantasy card game, a random mana level $m$ is drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201$. A player selects a random spell costing an integer amount of mana between $1$ and $m$, chosen uniformly at random. What is the expected cost of the spell selected?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208279 1071 "In a fantasy card game, a random mana level $m$ is drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201.$ A player selects a random spell costing an integer amount of mana between $1$ and $m,$ chosen uniformly at random. What is the expected cost of the spell selected?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208279 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a mana level $m$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $201$, and then a spell is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $m$. We are asked to find the expected value of the mana cost selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, the mana level drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201$.\n- Let $X$ be the cost of the spell selected, uniformly from $1$ to $Y$.\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$. Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y$, we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y = m$, the value of $X$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, m\\}$, so\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = m] = \\frac{m + 1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\frac{1 + Y}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot E[1 + Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 + E[Y])\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, 201\\}$, we compute\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\frac{1 + 201}{2} = 101\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 + 101) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 102 \\\\\n&= 51\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208279 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a mana level $m$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $201,$ and then a spell is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $m.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the mana cost selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, the mana level drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the cost of the spell selected, uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\textrm{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y = m,$ the value of $X$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, m\\},$ so\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = m] = \\frac{m + 1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\frac{1 + Y}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, 201\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\frac{1 + 201}{2} = 101.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+101) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =51.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208560 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531605 13194139535830 1759