\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=5$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.7,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.3+8\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.5+5.6 \\\\  & =7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208941 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a$, $b$, and $c$ with probabilities $0.25$, $0.5$, and $0.25$, respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$E[X∣Y = a] = 10, E[X∣Y = b] = 4, E[X∣Y = c] = 6$.\n\nWhat is $E[X]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208941 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ with probabilities $0.25,$ $0.5,$ and $0.25,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=10,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]=6.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208941 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=10$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\n- $Y=c$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]=6$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]\\cdot P(Y=c) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+6\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =6.0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208943 1071 "A surveyor is sent to one of three cities: $A$, $B$, or $C$. She is sent to City $A$ with a probability of $0.4$, City $B$ with a probability of $0.35$, and City $C$ with a probability of $0.25$.\n\nIf the expected number of households visited is $12$ in City $A$, $18$ in City $B$, and $10$ in City $C$, what is the expected number of households visited on a given day?\n\nThe expected number of households visited is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208943 1071 "A surveyor is sent to one of three cities: $A,$ $B,$ or $C.$ She is sent to City $A$ with a probability of $0.4,$ City $B$ with a probability of $0.35,$ and City $C$ with a probability of $0.25.$  \n\nIf the expected number of households visited is $12$ in City $A,$ $18$ in City $B,$ and $10$ in City $C,$ what is the expected number of households visited on a given day?\n\nThe expected number of households visited is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208943 1074 "We are given the probabilities of being assigned to one of three cities and the expected number of households visited in each. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which tells us how to compute the overall expected value by conditioning on which city the surveyor is sent to.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of households visited in a day, and let $Y$ be the city to which the surveyor is sent.\n\n- $Y=A$ with probability $0.4,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]=12.$\n\n- $Y=B$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]=18.$\n\n- $Y=C$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=C]=10.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]\\cdot P(Y=A)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]\\cdot P(Y=B) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=C]\\cdot P(Y=C) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 0.4+18\\cdot 0.35+10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =4.8+6.3+2.5 \\\\  & =13.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208944 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $E[X∣Y] = 2Y + 1$. If $E[Y] = 3$, what is $E[X]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208944 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208944 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y+1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208945 1071 "A game randomly selects an integer $n$ from $1$ to $301$, with each value equally likely. Then, a player is asked to guess an integer uniformly and randomly from $1$ to $n$. What is the expected value of the player’s guess?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208945 1071 "A game randomly selects an integer $n$ from $1$ to $301,$ with each value equally likely. Then, a player is asked to guess an integer uniformly and randomly from $1$ to $n.$ What is the expected value of the player’s guess?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208945 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $301,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the number selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $301.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the player’s guess, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{1+Y}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,301\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+301}{2}=151.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+151) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 152 \\\\  & =76.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208946 1071 "A video game randomly selects a level difficulty $d$ from $1$ to $500$, with each value equally likely. Once the difficulty is chosen, a treasure chest is placed in one of $d$ zones within the level, numbered from $1$ to $d$, with each zone equally likely to receive the chest.\n\nWhat is the expected number of the zone where the chest appears?\n\nThe expected number of the zone where the chest appears is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208946 1071 "A video game randomly selects a level difficulty $d$ from $1$ to $500,$ with each value equally likely. Once the difficulty is chosen, a treasure chest is placed in one of $d$ zones within the level, numbered from $1$ to $d,$ with each zone equally likely to receive the chest.\n\nWhat is the expected number of the zone where the chest appears?\n\nThe expected number of the zone where the chest appears is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208946 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a level difficulty is selected uniformly from the integers $1$ through $500,$ and then a treasure chest is placed uniformly in one of the zones numbered $1$ through $d,$ where $d$ is the selected difficulty.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the difficulty level of the chosen level. Since the difficulty is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $500,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,500\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of the zone where the chest is placed, which is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=d,$ the zone is selected uniformly from $1$ to $d.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=d]=\\frac{d+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,500\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+500}{2}=250.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (250.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 251.5 \\\\  & =125.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208947 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $E[X∣Y] = 5Y - 4$. If $E[X] = 11$, what is $E[Y]$?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186208947 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=5Y-4.$ If $\\text{E}[X]=11,$ what is $\\text{E}[Y]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186208947 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[5Y-4].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[5Y-4] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=11,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4 \\\\ 15 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186209885 1064 "We’ve seen that the law of total expectation allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable $X$ by conditioning on another variable $Y$. This idea extends naturally to a more general and elegant identity called the **tower property**:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this may look abstract, so let’s unpack what it means.\n\nThe inner term $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ is itself a *random variable*—its value depends on the outcome of $Y$. The outer expectation then takes the average of that conditional expectation over all possible values of $Y$.\n\nIn other words, we are averaging $X$ in two stages:\n\n- Within each scenario (for each possible value of $Y$), we find the expected value of $X$.\n- Across all scenarios, we take the weighted average of those conditional expectations, where the weights are the probabilities of each $Y$.\n\nLet’s explore a few examples to build some intuition." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186209885 1064 "We’ve seen that the law of total expectation allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable $X$ by conditioning on another variable $Y.$ This idea extends naturally to a more general and elegant identity called the **tower property**:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this may look abstract, so let’s unpack what it means.\n\nThe inner term $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ is itself a *random variable*—its value depends on the outcome of $Y.$ The outer expectation then takes the average of that conditional expectation over all possible values of $Y.$\n\nIn other words, we are averaging $X$ in two stages:\n\n- Within each scenario (for each possible value of $Y$), we find the expected value of $X.$\n\n- Across all scenarios, we take the weighted average of those conditional expectations, where the weights are the probabilities of each $Y.$\n\nLet’s explore a few examples to build some intuition." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186209886 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $Y$ takes only two values $y_1$ and $y_2$. We want to show that the *law of total expectation* holds in this simple case:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[X] = \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of expectation, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nNext, we express\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nusing the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the expectation formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\left(P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now distribute the sum across the two terms\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = P(Y = y_1) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) + P(Y = y_2) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nBut each of the sums above is just a conditional expectation according to the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(x)x \\cdot P(X = x∣Y = y_{1}) &= E[X∣Y = y_{1}] \\\\\n∑_(x)x \\cdot P(X = x∣Y = y_{2}) &= E[X∣Y = y_{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the more general case, where $Y$ takes more than two values." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186209886 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $Y$ takes only two values $y_1$ and $y_2.$  We want to show that the *law of total expectation* holds in this simple case:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[X] = \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of expectation, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x).\n$$\n\nNext, we express $P(X = x)$ using the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the expectation formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\left( P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2) \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now distribute the sum across the two terms\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = P(Y = y_1) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) + P(Y = y_2) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2).\n$$\n\nBut each of the sums above is just a conditional expectation according to the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x}x\\cdot P(X=x∣Y={y}_{1}) & =\\text{E}[X∣Y={y}_{1}] \\\\ {\\sum }_{x}x\\cdot P(X=x∣Y={y}_{2}) & =\\text{E}[X∣Y={y}_{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)},\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the more general case, where $Y$ takes more than two values." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186209887 1064 "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable, the expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{x} x\\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nThere is an alternative method for computing $\\textrm{E}[X]$, which allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable by conditioning on another variable.\n\nSuppose another discrete random variable $Y$ takes on a set of possible values. If we know\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nfor each value $y$, then the **law of total expectation** states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nConceptually, this tells us that $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is just a weighted average of the conditional expectations\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nwhere each weight is the probability\n\n$$\nP(Y = y)\n$$\n\nIn many problems, directly computing $\\textrm{E}[X]$ from the definition can be difficult. It turns out that breaking $\\textrm{E}[X]$ up into conditional pieces often simplifies things. The law of total expectation is especially useful when $X$ depends on an underlying condition $Y$, and each conditional case is easier to analyze than the full distribution of $X$.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice with a few examples." 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186209887 1064 "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable, the expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{x} x\\cdot P(X = x).\n$$\n\nThere is an alternative method for computing $\\textrm{E}[X],$ which allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable by conditioning on another variable.  \n\nSuppose another discrete random variable $Y$ takes on a set of possible values. If we know $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]$ for each value $y,$ then the **law of total expectation** states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y).\n$$\n\n \n\nConceptually, this tells us that $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is just a weighted average of the conditional expectations $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y],$ where each weight is the probability $P(Y = y).$  \n\nIn many problems, directly computing $\\textrm{E}[X]$ from the definition can be difficult. It turns out that breaking $\\textrm{E}[X]$ up into conditional pieces often simplifies things. The law of total expectation is especially useful when $X$ depends on an underlying condition $Y,$ and each conditional case is easier to analyze than the full distribution of $X.$\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice with a few examples." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186209888 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ with probabilities $0.4$ and $0.6$, respectively. Suppose we are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nLet's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the law of total expectation.\n\nThe law of total expectation states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]= \\sum_y \\textrm{E} [X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y = \\alpha$ with probability $0.4$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n- $Y = \\beta$ with probability $0.6$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[X∣Y = α] \\cdot P(Y = α) + E[X∣Y = β] \\cdot P(Y = β) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 0.4 + 3 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 2.8 + 1.8 \\\\\n&= 4.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186209888 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ with probabilities $0.4$ and $0.6,$ respectively. Suppose we are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nLet's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the law of total expectation.\n\nThe law of total expectation states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]= \\sum_y \\textrm{E} [X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y = \\alpha$ with probability $0.4,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n\n- $Y = \\beta$ with probability $0.6,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\alpha ]\\cdot P(Y=\\alpha )+\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\beta ]\\cdot P(Y=\\beta ) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8+1.8 \\\\  & =4.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186209889 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables. Now, we want to prove that the tower property holds:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of expectation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the law of total probability to express\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nin terms of $Y$. Let $S_Y$ denote the support of $Y$. Then, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(X = x \\mid Y = y) \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expectation formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\left(\\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(X = x \\mid Y = y) \\cdot P(Y = y) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now switch the order of summation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(Y = y) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y)\n$$\n\nEach inner sum is just the conditional expectation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y) = \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nBut this expression is just the definition of $\\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]$ for discrete $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y =