tes that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[\\sqrt{3}Y + 1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot E[Y] + E[1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 false] [17592186209955 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = \\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1.\n$$\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sqrt3,$ let's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$. \n\nThis is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186235407 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the circle ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{y}^