55 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531271 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531341 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531555 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531555 1077 "selection" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531555 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531341 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531555 1079 #uuid "ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531301 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531556 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531556 1077 "field-7" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531556 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531301 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531556 1079 #uuid "ff58c76f-d503-592c-8830-047604f3389f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531540 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1069 "q-72691" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1071 "Chantelle did an experiment in her Chemistry class. She heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-{5.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid increased by ${24.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$, at which point she decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1072 #uuid "02fcb6dd-d8d1-548e-be72-9006c65abdf0" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531557 1074 "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the variation in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-{5.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}+{24.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}={19.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was ${19.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531456 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531510 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1069 "q-215907" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=6$  and $2x+3y=2$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1072 #uuid "0e71161b-a139-5715-8ffb-5d9680416a1c" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531558 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y=18 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531547 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1069 "q-68484" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1071 "The city of Mexicali, in Mexico, is $6.6$ feet below sea level, while the city of Porto Alegre, in Brazil, is $55.8$ feet above sea level. What is the difference between the elevations of both cities?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1072 #uuid "0f0c1499-18ff-5c0e-854a-a4a1f5ed7366" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531559 1074 "To calculate the difference between the elevations of both cities, we must calculate the difference between the city with the highest elevation and the city with the lowest elevation. That is, we must calculate $55.8-(-6.6).$ This gives\n\n$$\n55.8-(-6.6)=55.8+6.6=62.4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531537 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1069 "q-275576" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1071 "In a puzzle game, a room's difficulty level $n$ is selected randomly from $1$ to $120,$ with each value equally likely. Inside each room are $n$ puzzle pieces numbered from $1$ to $n,$ and one of them is selected uniformly at random. What is the expected value of the number on the selected puzzle piece?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1072 #uuid "12dc9838-1006-5e99-98b8-8a2304014d14" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531560 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a room of difficulty level $n$ is chosen randomly and uniformly from $1$ to $120,$ and then a puzzle piece with a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the puzzle piece selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the room difficulty level, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $120.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the puzzle piece number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,120\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{120+1}{2}=60.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (60.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 61.5 \\\\  & =30.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531513 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1069 "q-103627" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1071 "$\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1072 #uuid "15a43dbb-9c91-5be9-a586-59bfd5235547" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531561 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x}{6}-\\frac{3y}{6}+\\frac{8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot y+\\frac{8}{6}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{y}{2}+\\frac{4z}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531447 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1069 "q-99756" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1071 "Find the value of $105÷0.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1072 #uuid "17babc52-289e-5d9d-bc9c-baaf511d4b6f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531562 1074 "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $105÷0$ is undefined." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531391 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531444 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531457 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1069 "q-269811" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1072 #uuid "19d96d91-ae11-5a12-a4f6-96b41a2e839b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531563 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -9 \\\\ 13 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531455 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531473 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1069 "q-215456" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1071 "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $4x+y=a$ and $7x+2y=-2$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(-2,b).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1072 #uuid "1afc104b-55b0-5a4c-8495-1467f6f4a4e0" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531564 1074 "Since $(-2,b)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=-2,\\,y=b$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=a \\\\ 7x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=-2,\\,y=b$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+b=a \\\\ 7(-2)+2b=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8+b=a \\\\ b=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we know that $b=6.$ Then, we can substitute $b=6$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+b & =a \\\\ -8+6 & =a \\\\ -2 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=6.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531469 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1069 "q-9541" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1071 "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1072 #uuid "1bd5daf3-8ee2-5ee2-ae53-b882ce969a04" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531565 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531406 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1069 "q-270277" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1072 #uuid "1c1ab071-19e5-52c5-b033-ca671fa83041" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531566 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531416 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1069 "q-77866" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$\n\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1072 #uuid "1d671ef3-a015-5e44-a360-62d57b505322" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531567 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is not defined at $x=\\pm 1,$ hence, it is not continuous at $x=\\pm 1,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531511 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1069 "q-119283" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1071 "The growth rate of a watermelon production $P(t)$ in a certain land is jointly proportional to the watermelon production at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the land's carrying capacity and the watermelon production. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2$ and the maximum possible watermelon production is $1\\,800,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1072 #uuid "2014fae0-a89c-5d1e-8735-16f0159dd06c" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531568 1074 "Since the watermelon production growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the watermelon production at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the watermelon production, the watermelon production follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1\\,800.$ Therefore, the watermelon production is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(1\\,800-P).\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531491 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1069 "q-77862" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4]?$\n\nI. $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$\n\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nIII. $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1072 #uuid "225c5ace-861b-5b33-b01d-abc5c6cb132b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531569 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531429 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1069 "q-12345" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1072 #uuid "2377eeaf-5209-5b0e-a180-97a6667f01fb" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531570 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=4x+3 \\\\ y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=4x+3$ into the second equation $y=-x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & =-x-2 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=-x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-2 \\\\  & =-(-1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1$ and $y=-1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-1).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531515 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1069 "q-2343" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ on the interval $[0,4].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1072 #uuid "25541256-5bc3-5680-8cfb-415ab2841d24" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531571 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}-2\\sqrt{0}}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =1 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531428 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1069 "q-3517" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1071 "$\\frac{11p{q}^{2}}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{2}}{121{p}^{4}q}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1072 #uuid "2867a50e-2edc-52ac-9829-fb91f9cd04d7" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531572 1074 "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator of one fraction and a denominator of the other one. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11p{q}^{2}}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{2}}{121{p}^{4}q} & =\\frac{11pq\\cdot q}{r\\cdot {r}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot {r}^{2}}{11pq\\cdot 11{p}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{11pq}\\cdot q}{r\\cdot \\cancel{{r}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{{r}^{2}}}{\\cancel{11pq}\\cdot 11{p}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{q}{r}\\cdot \\frac{5}{11{p}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5q}{11{p}^{3}r}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531452 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531489 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1069 "q-336597" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{-4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1072 #uuid "29e3e5d7-28ea-5e8a-b874-caa92a5ebba1" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531573 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4t}\\cdot (-2{e}^{2t})-0\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531529 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1069 "q-352098" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1071 "![](images/7d/7d6ef1d9a58af96b51152ce354ec85c616921187ed467273e8c41c4fe93fc19c.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1072 #uuid "2d1d4e63-93d8-5dc2-a21f-3d16bb6f6063" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531574 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{25}-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{125}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{100}-\\frac{1}{150}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531464 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531472 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1069 "q-332913" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1072 #uuid "2e73d348-8d9b-5a9f-8dbf-5305290f7f53" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531575 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -3[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(\\frac{2b-1}{3})+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531399 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1069 "q-126111" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1071 "![](images/28/287bde55681f44bd4132fdd5b4777a66d3dba7b16708533d755379c41f35b797.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1072 #uuid "2fefe340-5c6b-584d-b7e8-9e9204c610ea" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531576 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(-3y+4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3}+4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{1}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})+4(\\frac{27}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-12+\\frac{104}{3}-20) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531400 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531414 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531506 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1069 "q-269795" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1072 #uuid "316244d1-8305-5968-b730-d6310a788240" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531577 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531415 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531496 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531551 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1069 "q-333012" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    $2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    $1$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1072 #uuid "3234de52-8801-51a2-806f-23f965c5de2f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531578 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-{a}_{2}+2){t}^{2}+(-{b}_{2}+1)t+(-{c}_{2}+3) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}+2)t+{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{2}+2=0 \\\\ -\\,{b}_{2}+1=2{a}_{1} \\\\ -\\,{c}_{2}+3={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}+2=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and fourth equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and fifth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last values into the third and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=1,\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+2t-1 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531516 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1069 "q-30282" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1071 "![](images/9a/9a448574651a74ef7180549827e05ee2d790cd9fa89c6c85adf08d8444ceaadf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({10}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1072 #uuid "33e5e3ff-cf8e-5556-9914-9fd8f6506ea2" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531579 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({10}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({10}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({10}^{x})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k({10}^{0})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 1 & =k-3 \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4({10}^{x})-3.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531478 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1069 "q-14411" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4{z}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{8xz}{y}?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1072 #uuid "375b774b-1afa-5119-93c1-a64d09ffe7d5" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531580 1074 "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4{z}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{8xz}{y} & =\\frac{5y\\cdot y}{4z\\cdot z}\\cdot \\frac{4z\\cdot 2x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y\\cdot \\cancel{y}}{\\cancel{4z}\\cdot z}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{4z}\\cdot 2x}{\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{z}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10xy}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531528 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1069 "q-332750" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2/5 & -6/5 \\\\ -6/5 & -7/5\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1072 #uuid "38450a0f-4d09-5bde-a910-db2e5f9dab01" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531581 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531507 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1069 "q-74587" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1071 "![](images/9a/9a1f9f1877ae0ecbc37464c5b8b20821070e7927e59dea8d930ceb8f37313722.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1072 #uuid "3ba51678-bfa5-5eea-a51d-29e6a8b3feb3" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531582 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a parallelogram since it has $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. It has no pairs of opposite sides that are parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shape $S$ is not parallelogram." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531422 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531470 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531493 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531524 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531534 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1069 "q-270266" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}^{3}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{5} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1072 #uuid "3cd8cc0b-abb0-561c-b8fb-73430e8105db" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531583 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531451 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1069 "q-138069" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1072 #uuid "3e4f6268-3e58-5684-9bea-f874fd53aaf3" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531584 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531384 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531403 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1069 "q-215425" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $4x+3y=5$ and $-x-6y=4$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1072 #uuid "3f0c0760-2f71-5da7-a09a-417c59aab4e2" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531585 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ -x-6y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+6y=10 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+\\cancel{6y} & =10 \\\\ -x-\\cancel{6y} & =4 \\\\ (8x-x) & =(10+4) \\\\ 7x & =14 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $4x+3y=5$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =5 \\\\ 4(2)+3y & =5 \\\\ 8+3y & =5 \\\\ 3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=2,\\,y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(2,-1).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531459 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1069 "q-101566" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1071 "What number can be added to $-35$ to get a sum of $0?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1072 #uuid "42762342-58bc-5c22-b022-fb45988d86d1" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531586 1074 "The number that can be added to $-35$ to give $0$ is the additive inverse of $-35.$\n\nTo find the additive inverse of the number $-35$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\,(-35)=35.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-35)+35=-35+35=0\\,✓\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531458 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1069 "q-138980" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1072 #uuid "42788b85-30ff-5f36-a9fd-86e424b97617" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531587 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531495 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1069 "q-42849" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1071 "$240÷(-12)=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1072 #uuid "43993959-de6b-5c51-8ec3-a8748bbd2836" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531588 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(240)$ by a negative one $(-12).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n240÷(-12)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $240$ by $12$ without any signs: $240÷12=20.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n240÷(-12)=-20.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531387 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531413 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531432 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531436 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531466 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531522 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1069 "q-268179" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1072 #uuid "43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531589 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531390 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531424 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531433 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531462 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531512 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531514 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531542 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531546 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1069 "q-332587" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1072 #uuid "48e63c6e-e110-5f2f-9c73-cb4374b7c6c6" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531590 1074 "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-4)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531504 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1069 "q-101460" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1071 "![](images/de/de806ae4a790c7339d49e362204a7a16257a3cb43165e0992f9a35afffed01bd.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ hexagons." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1072 #uuid "4b671a86-bc70-5855-bc03-8c1fbe9580d0" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531591 1074 "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $10$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $2:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:5$ is equivalent to $2:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:5$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $5$ hexagons.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531492 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1069 "q-104220" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1072 #uuid "551dfc8f-f76b-5f91-a118-1f244aa7dd9d" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531592 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $5,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35b6843ace65d2261f0910fbeae0e64383e7e325e3b851652b3aa421e311094.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531509 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1069 "q-60651" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1071 "![](images/c9/c9b42a179625b667cb54f1b597127809d851a1b7a830a2632b09402ecb790330.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,2)$  and $(-2,2)?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1072 #uuid "553e8be5-17eb-5fb4-be35-fa84cdea996b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531593 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/49/498674b4a4667b57be1574a081bbb948350754ed8993c14428ad8f31ee17bd90.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/f3/f3811804824e802c4cb20af85bac7f21f6c4e0f60e0430607c876e1cf27b7cd3.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(3,2)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $2+3=5.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531394 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531409 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531411 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531477 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531521 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531530 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531533 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531556 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1069 "q-332576" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+5{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+6{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,{e}^{5t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1072 #uuid "56f780de-76f1-5a1a-81ae-abc4bc467c3f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531594 1074 "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531418 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1069 "q-275578" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1071 "A robot starts at the bottom of a staircase with $n$ steps, where $n$ is an integer chosen uniformly at random from $1$ to $10.$ The robot then climbs a uniformly random number of steps between $1$ and $n.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of steps the robot climbs?\n\nThe expected number of steps the robot climbs is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1072 #uuid "5923cf06-a8dd-57e8-81ff-5f4a640b9a2b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531595 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $10,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number: the number of steps in the staircase. Since an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $10,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,10\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of steps the robot climbs, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,10\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{10+1}{2}=5.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6.5 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531519 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531536 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531539 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1069 "q-336703" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1072 #uuid "59d9272b-7eb2-549a-868b-86d14c7fdef5" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531596 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531405 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531438 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531497 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531531 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531552 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1069 "q-269762" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}+1 \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1072 #uuid "5b9f6727-3d7f-553c-90bd-4d6b87d491ad" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531597 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 4$ matrix is a $2\\times 4$ matrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531446 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531484 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531550 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1069 "q-269782" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1072 #uuid "5bd6ce37-dfc0-53de-b48f-f0c1cbf86c1e" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531598 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2}-{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531437 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1069 "q-126100" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1071 "![](images/cc/cc7b2e67a5137b32917d8b96e3a53f66266e3ecabc2738b2a42417e87664457b.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1072 #uuid "5e9d38c5-007b-59fb-ae42-189521635fd6" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531599 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,\\,3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531396 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531505 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531517 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531527 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1069 "q-269804" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,0]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1072 #uuid "622e64f1-e6e7-5b08-85d4-13a13c5f884f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531600 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 13 \\\\ 6 & -15 \\\\ 16 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531460 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1069 "q-113355" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1071 "$\\frac{{w}^{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3z}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1072 #uuid "6455b841-9f87-516b-8fea-0d225062fdb2" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531601 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{w}^{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3z}=\\frac{{w}^{3}\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 3z}=\\frac{5{w}^{3}}{6z}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531501 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1069 "q-363229" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=5y-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1072 #uuid "69689ccc-dfd4-5812-991b-d48dbc7eafee" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531602 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 5y(1-{y}^{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(5y-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{y}^{2}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}(1-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(1-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531508 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1069 "q-344426" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1071 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1072 #uuid "6a23334e-1f04-5511-928e-2caa0a2313aa" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531603 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-4.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and the endpoints $x=0,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints)\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & ={0}^{2}-4(0)+5=5 \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5=1 \\\\ f(5) & ={5}^{2}-4(5)+5=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=10,$ so the global maximum is $10.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a52ee757248f6b742af36af1e6af9e801cb8e5276b119e399b6fff36d75d0dd.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531541 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1069 "q-26914" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1072 #uuid "6bfc4a3c-5e7b-532b-95bd-4e49a1efa533" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531604 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/6d/6d5b359544227b89d1dc5ca9967c9dfcda84bf5345184063a1d0deef994c3692.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86b10c71101ae46d4c99335874eb3cd75abc416c0d9a3b4552f6f6a52644aa2f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,8),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/05/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1069 "q-275428" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $p,$ $q,$ and $r$ with probabilities $0.5,$ $0.3,$ and $0.2,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1072 #uuid "6d436b5a-22ca-50fe-bbca-e3c952622b7f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531605 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=p$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8$\n\n- $Y=q$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14$\n\n- $Y=r$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]\\cdot P(Y=p)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]\\cdot P(Y=q) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]\\cdot P(Y=r) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.5+14\\cdot 0.3+10\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4+4.2+2 \\\\  & =10.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531395 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1069 "q-353601" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1071 "![](images/ec/ec0b88ec00ef7e7061af0b08761eb73f05a5f669208babacbd4eb86736019230.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=6{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1072 #uuid "6ddfe515-d085-541a-8dab-b3a5ab40f555" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531606 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=18{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(18{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{36{e}^{6\\theta }+324{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{360{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{[{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531482 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531502 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1069 "q-215449" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1071 "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $y=-2x+2$ and $y=-5x-a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b,8).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1072 #uuid "6f0810d4-95d5-5ec1-a80a-fff5b7ccd8fa" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531607 1074 "Since $(b,8)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b,\\,y=8$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+2 \\\\ y=-5x-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b,\\,y=8$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2(b)+2 \\\\ 8=-5(b)-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2b+2 \\\\ 8=-5b-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $b=-3.$ Then, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-5b-a \\\\ 8 & =-5(-3)-a \\\\ 8 & =15-a \\\\ -7 & =-a \\\\ 7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=-3.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531439 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1069 "q-155031" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1071 "![](images/97/97faa2ca35485f76f012475a9ba2b6af7938cfc86728bddd560cf3da59fff197.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1072 #uuid "73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531608 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/f5/f52592db0fee86e26318d6d2de9d7f685f1fb9f5d416628a04878857f1c54b54.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/76/76f0da764caf0237a2d30d32d593886d8df6c348368fe889f7447be781b6faf1.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/97/9778abd955cdc9b705800eba8f3d0d635af1c6a5fd5724583bdb32a943c6d44f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531388 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1069 "q-98377" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number\n\nII. $\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number\n\nIII. $5$ is a rational number" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1072 #uuid "76ebc4d7-7b51-5dea-a66f-038cad1e5104" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531609 1074 "A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{(-3)}{4}.$\n\n- $\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number.\n\n- $5$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{5}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531463 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1069 "q-14412" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1071 "Given that $\\frac{mn-4n}{2m}=a$ and $\\frac{2}{m-4}=b$, what is $a\\cdot b?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1072 #uuid "77bb223c-233d-57ea-b08b-423b072f4e97" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531610 1074 "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =\\frac{mn-4n}{2m}\\cdot \\frac{2}{m-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{n(m-4)}{2\\cdot m}\\cdot \\frac{2}{m-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\cancel{(m-4)}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot m}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{m-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531392 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531538 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531553 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1069 "q-270274" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1072 #uuid "78eabbdb-ae14-5218-80c9-de54367d95a9" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531611 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{3} & 3{y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531421 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1069 "q-352100" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1071 "![](images/69/69dbf7a007980f1f1a9c2531ad398237e22d28f329afc619e05b8372afe970d4.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1072 #uuid "7e282fab-f673-50f6-9e1f-aaa485da50b0" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531612 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-2y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,1-2y=0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y({y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}({y}^{3}-2{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{16}-0)-\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{80}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{320}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531523 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1069 "q-18519" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1071 "![](images/95/9507e060ddd7de93489b236b48856870e2c884eba69f63ac67662dfd1c061c17.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1072 #uuid "8412e9ee-2ea9-5393-944f-a1233b5c49f8" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531613 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ so $c=1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k({2}^{0})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k(1)+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.5({2}^{x})+1.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531554 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1069 "q-269816" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1072 #uuid "84a8217d-9b8f-5f74-ba0c-1dc1b7379e21" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531614 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531483 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1069 "q-332746" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]){e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1072 #uuid "86e2d08a-1bef-5ead-ab02-f3370f825b7b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531615 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531389 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1069 "q-275442" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1071 "An order picker in a warehouse is assigned to either Section $1$ or Section $2$ on any given day. With probability $0.65,$ the order picker works in Section $1,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $120.$ With probability $0.35,$ the order picker works in Section $2,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $95.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of orders picked per day?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1072 #uuid "87aec959-895e-5be8-8c69-b3c4045c5829" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531616 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of orders picked per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the section to which the picker is assigned.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of orders picked, and let $Y$ be the warehouse section to which the picker is assigned.\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.65,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=120.$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=95.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.65+95\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =78+33.25 \\\\  & =111.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531453 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1069 "q-128432" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1071 "Expressed as a fraction, $(-13)÷(-5)=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1072 #uuid "8839a979-8588-586f-ba7f-29f7bd102689" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531617 1074 "We have to divide a negative number $(-13)$ by another negative number $(-5).$ So the result will be positive:\n\n$$\n(-13)÷(-5)=\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $13$ by $5$ without any signs: $13÷5=\\frac{13}{5}.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n(-13)÷(-5)=\\frac{13}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531423 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1069 "q-181155" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1071 "$56÷(-8)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1072 #uuid "89a1fe1c-2eda-5608-9d61-d01a69e48b0c" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531618 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(56)$ by a negative one $(-8).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n56÷(-8)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $56$ by $8$ without any signs: $56÷8=7.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n56÷(-8)=-7.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531448 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531499 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1069 "q-215406" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1072 #uuid "8fea67f7-ec4c-5ab9-8c67-4d43507c8b87" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531619 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+2 \\\\ y=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531426 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1069 "q-269831" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1072 #uuid "92b16d62-78ea-5a5a-bcd9-f319e1b69729" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531620 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-19 & 25 \\\\ 9 & -11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531481 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1069 "q-138022" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1072 #uuid "957651b3-45e4-5a93-853b-1785322d0942" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531621 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}^{2}\\le {x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  in turn, $Q(x)\\le 1.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $1.$ But before we conclude that $1$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=1.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $1.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531525 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1069 "q-105681" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1071 "![](images/5a/5a9564ba5a69f4bafa8ff85275ee009b133101aa36c682dd3e50efc949c76d92.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-4+5y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1072 #uuid "95c12fb3-5acd-5573-a477-4562c842d9b2" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531622 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,\\,4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531544 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1069 "q-70318" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1071 "$\\frac{7}{a-5}\\cdot \\frac{b{c}^{3}}{3}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1072 #uuid "98c5a09c-66f3-5ba5-a68c-81bc86cd79e6" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531623 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{a-5}\\cdot \\frac{b{c}^{3}}{3}=\\frac{7\\cdot b{c}^{3}}{(a-5)\\cdot 3}=\\frac{7b{c}^{3}}{3(a-5)}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531450 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1069 "q-275544" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y-2.$ If $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ what is $\\text{E}[Y]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1072 #uuid "9a2b9353-2f5e-55ce-b33e-819639da3c0b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531624 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y-2].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y-2] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2 \\\\ 10 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531385 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1069 "q-362710" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1071 "![](images/3d/3dbd6e325acdddddfb0bdcca5eef77fc3e75df2ccf946592780a0e1043ee1b01.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=1$ (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1072 #uuid "a00b0b94-53a1-5e23-84f6-859ac793fc56" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531625 1074 "The region is enclosed between the $y$-axis, the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, the width of the cylindrical shells is given by $f(y)=0-(-\\sqrt{y}+y)=\\sqrt{y}-y.$ Using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\sqrt{y}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3/2}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =2\\pi ((\\frac{2}{5}-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531443 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1069 "q-103828" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1071 "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1072 #uuid "a1c1460f-c5a2-5e99-b7f7-17db73e707c6" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531626 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531420 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531465 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531468 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1069 "q-269812" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,-2]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1072 #uuid "a62becc2-1daf-5292-96cb-be1f422032a1" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531627 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 23 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531427 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1069 "q-353582" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1071 "![](images/83/83813bff1ff86036e2773d9476150de619688daec9cae2623a555a43290cace3.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1072 #uuid "a9290c4e-ece3-5a58-aecc-bb62b4894116" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531628 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531494 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1069 "q-181157" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1071 "$80÷(-20)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1072 #uuid "aa0a4c98-4d7d-54fc-bc9e-2776a355d7bb" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531629 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(80)$ by a negative one $(-20).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n80÷(-20)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $80$ by $20$ without any signs: $80÷20=4.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n80÷(-20)=-4.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531479 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1069 "q-68925" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1071 "In Toronto, Canada, the average temperature in November is ${4.9}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ In December, the average temperature decreases by ${5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to November. Finally, in January, the average temperature decreases by ${3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to the previous month. Determine the average temperature in Toronto in January." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1072 #uuid "aab3a1fe-f42d-5861-9d8c-8da472abefec" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531630 1074 "First, we must determine the average temperature in Toronto during the month of December. Since we know that the temperature decreases on average by ${5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to November, we subtract the variation in temperature from the average temperature in November. This gives\n\n$$\n{4.9}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}-{5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}=-{0.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nNow, we must determine the temperature during the month of January. Since we know that the temperature decreases on average by ${3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to December, we subtract the variation in temperature from the average temperature in December. This gives\n\n$$\n-{0.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}-{3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}=-{3.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average temperature in Toronto in January is $-{3.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531440 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1069 "q-93027" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1071 "A water tank with a capacity of $5,000$ liters is filled with water to $42.5%$ of its capacity. How much water is in the tank?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1072 #uuid "aeb2c9f4-4f11-5f7d-ac23-e92ca6ecec4e" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531631 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $5,000$ liters. The percentage is $42.5%,$ and the part is the amount of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n42.5%=42.5÷100=0.425\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =0.425\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n42.5%=\\frac{42.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{42.5}{100}\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =42.5\\times \\frac{5,000}{100} \\\\  & =42.5\\times 50 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531407 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1069 "q-48398" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1072 #uuid "b1af75bd-8ab0-5a82-8a2c-c57f1e5066dc" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531632 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.5.$   Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531518 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1069 "q-269827" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1072 #uuid "b20e05f8-4c24-503b-a4d3-441eb6663ecb" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531633 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531461 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1069 "q-105733" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1072 #uuid "ba5c4320-47ed-5bb0-ac9c-df1a49799343" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531634 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -6+5y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =0 \\\\ -(y-2)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=2$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=3.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}y\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531498 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1069 "q-26915" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1072 #uuid "be426e06-620a-5f1c-9841-393e8911826d" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531635 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/84/8490920052778795ca03072fd0a6933b4abc5756f2e75ae9f61863d3726dd5c8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/b5/b5deca2c07bc8c8a5e58c28a6ca2449268999a54b21407380ae168aafabd7a5d.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6)$ to $(0,10),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/57/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png)" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531490 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1069 "q-70320" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1071 "$\\frac{4{s}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1072 #uuid "c11871ea-d169-5487-96ff-63c7ffae0586" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531636 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{s}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}}{t\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}{t}^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531488 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1069 "q-269825" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{y}_{1}+6{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1072 #uuid "c217a4e9-7d66-55f8-9bd7-c609e2aa5841" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531637 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 21 \\\\ -2 & -24\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531545 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1069 "q-104215" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1071 "![](images/aa/aa9c640445da2c47bfabe3afe2cacb2acd2e67e42e052260c1cdf87d5c81dd16.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1072 #uuid "c3cbf898-5adf-5f1a-9995-178ecdb7db19" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531638 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $2,$ we must have $z=2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=2.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531410 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1069 "q-223387" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $523$\n\nII. $532$\n\nIII. $501$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1072 #uuid "cca0b3f3-f64a-53b6-8329-0c693549bc28" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531639 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $523,$ we have that \n\n$$\n52-2(3)=52-6=46\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $523$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $532,$ we have that \n\n$$\n53-2(2)=53-4=49\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $532$ is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $501,$ we have that \n\n$$\n50-2(1)=50-2=48\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $501$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531383 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1069 "q-275520" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1071 "A salesperson visits one of three regions: North, South, or East. The probabilities that they are sent North, South, and East are $0.25,$ $0.5,$ and $0.25$ respectively.\n\nIf the expected number of sales made daily is $9$ in the North, $14$ in the South, and $11$ in the East, what is the expected number of sales made in a day?\n\nThe expected number of sales made is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1072 #uuid "cdbfe9c8-c3f2-5a6b-9ce7-99052b0abd68" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531640 1074 "We are given the probability of being sent to one of three regions and the expected number of sales made. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which tells us how to compute the overall expected value by conditioning on which region the salesperson visits.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sales made in a day, and let $Y$ be the region the salesperson visited.\n\n- $Y=\\text{North}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]=9.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{South}$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]=14.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{East}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]=11.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{North})+\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{South}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{East}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 0.25+14\\cdot 0.5+11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.25+7+2.75 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531386 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531398 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531402 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531404 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531476 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531549 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1069 "q-269758" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1072 #uuid "cfa20750-c86e-5358-ae72-96466e6a1578" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531641 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 4$ matrix is a $2\\times 4$ matrix." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531548 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1069 "q-275580" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1071 "A robotic arm randomly selects a box in a warehouse containing $n$ items from $1$ to $150,$ with each value equally likely. The arm then randomly picks one of these $n$ items and scans its position number (from $1$ to $n,$ each value equally likely).\n\nWhat is the expected position number scanned?\n\nThe expected position number scanned is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1072 #uuid "d15a80b9-3b38-5de0-953c-45a827aaaf2b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531642 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a box is selected containing $n$ items uniformly from $1$ through $150,$ and then an item is chosen uniformly in one of the positions numbered $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the number of items in the box. Since this is an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $150,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,150\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the position number scanned, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,150\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{150+1}{2}=75.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (75.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 76.5 \\\\  & =38.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531474 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1069 "q-275432" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ with probabilities $0.15,$ $0.55,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1072 #uuid "d3d68253-5058-5406-8e0e-23e4aaba826a" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531643 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=-1$ with probability $0.15,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4$\n\n- $Y=0$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9$\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]\\cdot P(Y=-1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+9\\cdot 0.55+8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6+4.95+2.4 \\\\  & =7.95\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531417 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1069 "q-9540" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1071 "![](images/c1/c1bf9d989f1fbaeda90325b924f2c9285cd4e37e1948ba63256258b0a84c7e44.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={2}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1072 #uuid "da080066-6980-5e46-a310-b96dc7f7120c" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531644 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531431 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531445 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531526 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1069 "q-270271" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1072 #uuid "dac59390-e4c1-5a03-ac45-73be8ac0d42f" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531645 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 1-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531503 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1069 "q-141915" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1071 "$(-5)(2x+3y)-5y=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1072 #uuid "dcca9682-5c6e-581c-bcc3-60c96a3dddcd" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531646 1074 "First, we distribute the $(-5)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(-5)(2x+3y)-5y & = \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 2x+(-5)\\cdot 3y-5y & = \\\\ (-10x)+(-15y)-5y & = \\\\ -10x-15y-5y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-10x-15y-5y & = \\\\ -10x+(-15y-5y) & = \\\\ -10x-20y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531430 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1069 "q-68888" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1071 "Jordan did an experiment in his Chemistry class. He heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-{1.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid increased by ${7.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$, at which point he decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1072 #uuid "de213b3e-d398-5228-8036-33cbe3a6bb3b" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531647 1074 "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the variation in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-{1.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}+{7.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}={6.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was ${6.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531419 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1069 "q-126096" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1071 "![](images/93/9312e042b08ce1aae28eab8bb64aff176fc734c38184d781e348dc5ec6058bfa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1072 #uuid "dfd26745-ef58-57dc-ac34-552acd1f12bb" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531648 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531467 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1069 "q-275440" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1071 "A tutoring agency assigns a tutor to either High School $A$ or High School $B.$ With probability $0.45,$ the tutor is sent to High School $A,$ where the expected number of students tutored in a day is $4.8.$ With probability $0.55,$ the tutor is sent to High School $B,$ where the expected number of students tutored is $6.2.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of students tutored in a day?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1072 #uuid "e0fafa34-8718-5c91-847f-90668c3f437e" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531649 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of students tutored per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the high school the tutor is assigned to.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of students tutored in a day, and let $Y$ be the school to which the tutor is assigned.\n\n- $Y=A$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]=4.8.$\n\n- $Y=B$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]=6.2.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]\\cdot P(Y=A)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]\\cdot P(Y=B) \\\\  & =4.8\\cdot 0.45+6.2\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.16+3.41 \\\\  & =5.57\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531442 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1069 "q-14403" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-5x+6}\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{x-4}?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1072 #uuid "e1404dec-45bb-593f-8444-817e3a0c6414" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531650 1074 "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-5x+6}\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{x-4} & =\\frac{(x+4)(x-4)}{(x-2)(x-3)}\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+4)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{(x-2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{x-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+4}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531475 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1069 "q-275443" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1071 "A ride-share driver starts every day in either the City Center or the Suburban Area. The driver starts the day in the City Center with probability $0.55,$ where the expected number of rides given per day is $14.3.$ The driver starts the day in the Suburban Area with probability $0.45,$ where the expected number of rides is $11.7.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of rides given in a day?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1072 #uuid "e734511f-0834-5433-966a-4ae3851cf360" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531651 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of rides given in a day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the driver's starting location.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rides completed in a day, and $Y$ be the location from which the driver started.\n\n- $Y=\\text{City}$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]=14.3.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{Suburban}$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]=11.7.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{City}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{Suburban}) \\\\  & =14.3\\cdot 0.55+11.7\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =7.865+5.265 \\\\  & =13.13\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531401 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531408 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531434 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531435 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531441 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531454 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531480 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531500 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1069 "q-332585" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1072 #uuid "eadcb6eb-28e0-561c-baf8-c3576858d2b7" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531652 1074 "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531543 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1069 "q-275531" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=4Y-3.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=10,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1072 #uuid "eff3dc19-9675-582f-bb2d-ae75355788e9" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531653 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[4Y-3]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[4Y-3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 10-3 \\\\  & =37\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531412 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1069 "q-70319" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1071 "$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}\\cdot \\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}=$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1072 #uuid "f3ac8b1a-7d85-55e8-a403-9ecc671851c3" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531654 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}\\cdot \\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot 6{y}^{2}}{2y\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}y} \\\\  & =3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying." 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531555 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1069 "q-155051" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1071 "![](images/01/01aba7550fb134b1f3a93276dc8f3b91794844ea21254c2c75a0702a443a60e3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1072 #uuid "f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531655 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/63/6310ff39996be529bc44e5058ef9710400f0c8089196a57e7f4eb541703e53ae.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/63/6334d2bc1fda6390b6ec542afb8cc37d4f76322b98d41ca6bbb3af3c8d735c8f.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/89/8939cff2d7cc8ca9a08c7be63517ffb900e49b769edc5c2454119f4da25904cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531425 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1069 "q-162057" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1071 "$(-5)÷4$ is equivalent to" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1072 #uuid "f462c87b-3e02-54b6-b206-e6def06b7c2e" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531656 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-5)$ by a positive one $(4).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5÷4=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531471 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1069 "q-155053" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1071 "![](images/a3/a31a793df705b6cbe8dddb648ca6a0d2ebb6f4eb724ff8b99682f48cafb072a0.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1072 #uuid "f8c7f0d6-8dc6-549e-8375-7c33cf7277e3" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531657 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/aa/aad13ab456bc61c90522725ddb60f481c6138a9e53a50136d691ddd344349cf1.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/14/143e831321a485a0e57b706c6df89273bd92b3134d691d1ef41a67b7f7076481.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c361eea2a598de063dc62375716ca5bdb7b0a06d4c30cf9a5c420cd9cc48e4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531520 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1069 "q-275424" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a$ and $b$ with probabilities $0.55$ and $0.45,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1072 #uuid "fb48c25d-7d3e-5c68-83ac-d6034fcc4a05" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531658 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.55+4\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =3.3+1.8 \\\\  & =5.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531449 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1069 "q-12341" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1072 #uuid "fbb8ab84-c16d-554a-bf88-2b57aaa4afbf" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531659 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+4 \\\\ y=3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531393 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531532 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531535 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1069 "q-270269" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1072 #uuid "fdb98924-99f5-55a4-8e7e-a090dae96539" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531660 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2} & 2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -24 \\\\ 24 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531397 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1069 "q-275538" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=\\sqrt{3},$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1072 #uuid "fde0d020-b26d-5edb-a043-de5b1f35d4a2" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186531661 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 tru